PARAGRAPH 1
To resolve the "Adafruit_GFX.h: No such file or directory" error, you must install the required libraries. Open the Arduino IDE, navigate to Sketch -> Include Library -> Manage Libraries, and search for "Adafruit GFX" and "Adafruit SSD1306". Install both.

PARAGRAPH 2
You must also install "Adafruit BusIO", as the display driver depends on it. Without this dependency, the SSD1306 library will fail to compile even if the GFX library is present.

PARAGRAPH 3
The provided code had a syntax error in the `setup()` function (an unclosed curly brace). I have corrected this. Additionally, the `dis()` function is now defined before `loop()` so the compiler can recognize it during the main execution block.

PARAGRAPH 4
To upload to the Pro Mini, first upload "ArduinoISP" to your Uno. Connect Uno 10 -> Pro Mini RST, Uno 11 -> MOSI, Uno 12 -> MISO, Uno 13 -> SCK. Ensure you connect GND and VCC correctly (respecting 3.3V/5V logic).

PARAGRAPH 5
In the IDE, select "Arduino Pro or Pro Mini", choose your Processor (ATmega328P 3.3V 8MHz is common), and select "Arduino as ISP" under Tools -> Programmer. Use "Sketch -> Upload Using Programmer".

PARAGRAPH 6
The following code is the corrected version. I have removed the incomplete `Serial` references and organized the initialization to be safe for the limited memory of the Pro Mini.

PARAGRAPH 7
```cpp
#include <Wire.h>
#include <Adafruit_GFX.h>
#include <Adafruit_SSD1306.h>

#define SCREEN_WIDTH 128
#define SCREEN_HEIGHT 32
